---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-muscle-cramps`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Muscle Cramps',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Muscle cramp', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'R25.2', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Muscle Cramps', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Muscle Cramps: Deficiency, Calcium, and What Helps"
  description="Muscle cramps have many causes. Where does vitamin D fit — through calcium handling and muscle function — and when is a cramp a sign of deficiency worth correcting?"
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-muscle-cramps", label: "Vitamin D & Muscle Cramps" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Muscle Cramps</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Muscle cramps — those sudden, involuntary, painful contractions, classically in the calf at
      night — are one of the most common reasons people wonder about a mineral or vitamin deficiency.
      Vitamin D does have a real role in muscle, but it is easy to over-attribute cramps to it. Here is
      where it genuinely fits and where it does not.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">How vitamin D affects muscle</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Muscle cells carry vitamin D receptors, and vitamin D influences calcium handling within the
      muscle fibre — the same calcium flux that governs contraction and relaxation. Severe deficiency
      causes a genuine <em>myopathy</em>: proximal muscle weakness, aching, and difficulty with stairs
      or rising from a chair, sometimes with cramping. When 25(OH)D is very low (the
      <a href="/vitamin-d-and-osteomalacia" class="text-amber-700 underline">osteomalacia</a> range),
      muscle symptoms are part of the picture, and correcting the deficiency reliably improves them.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">But most cramps aren't from vitamin D</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Ordinary nocturnal leg cramps in otherwise well people usually have nothing to do with vitamin D.
      Common contributors include dehydration, overexertion, prolonged standing, ageing, pregnancy,
      and medications (diuretics, statins, some asthma drugs). Electrolyte disturbances —
      <a href="/magnesium" class="text-amber-700 underline">magnesium</a>,
      <a href="/potassium" class="text-amber-700 underline">potassium</a>, calcium, and sodium — are
      more directly tied to cramping than vitamin D itself, and vitamin D matters partly <em>through</em>
      its effect on calcium. Trials of magnesium for idiopathic leg cramps, notably, have been largely
      disappointing, which is a useful reminder that "a supplement should fix this" often does not hold.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When to suspect deficiency</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Cramps <em>plus</em> genuine proximal weakness, bone pain, or difficulty climbing stairs.</li>
      <li>Risk factors for deficiency: little sun exposure, dark skin at high latitude, malabsorption, older age.</li>
      <li>Cramps that improve when a documented low 25(OH)D is corrected — the meaningful test.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical approach</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      For isolated night cramps, the basics come first: stay hydrated, stretch the calf before bed,
      review your medications, and address any electrolyte issues. If you have risk factors or muscle
      weakness alongside the cramps, checking and correcting a vitamin D deficiency is reasonable and
      may help. But do not assume a cramp automatically means you are low in vitamin D — most of the
      time it does not.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Frequent, severe, or one-sided cramps, or cramps with
      swelling, weakness, or numbness, deserve medical assessment to rule out circulation, nerve, or
      metabolic causes.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-muscle-cramps" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
